From: Cargt <tim.carney@cargt.com>
Subject: [PATCH] serial: stm32: fix pm_runtime race in break_ctl

stm32_usart_break_ctl() calls pm_runtime_get(), the non-blocking
variant, which only requests an async resume and returns immediately
without waiting for the clock/power domain to actually come back up.
It then immediately takes the port lock and writes USART_RQR_SBKRQ
directly to hardware. If the port has already autosuspended, this
races a still-in-progress resume and can hit the peripheral before
its clock is back on, producing a synchronous external abort:

  Internal error: synchronous external abort: 0000000096000210 [#1] PREEMPT SMP
  ...
  pc : stm32_usart_break_ctl+0x48/0x98
  Call trace:
   stm32_usart_break_ctl+0x48/0x98
   uart_break_ctl+0x4c/0x78
   ttyport_break_ctl+0x28/0x3c
   serdev_device_break_ctl+0x24/0x38
   ps_control+0x68/0x198 [btnxpuart]
   ps_work_func+0x50/0x74 [btnxpuart]

Observed triggered from btnxpuart's power-save workqueue, which calls
serdev_device_break_ctl() asynchronously with no other guarantee the
port is already resumed - unlike the port's normal read/write paths,
which are only reached while a user session keeps the device
continuously active.

This pm_runtime handling in break_ctl is a Cargt/ST vendor addition on
top of mainline (mainline's stm32_usart_break_ctl(), introduced in
30e945861f3b ("serial: stm32: add support for break control"), has
never had any pm_runtime calls in it at all) - no upstream fix exists
to pull in for this, so switch this one call site to the blocking
pm_runtime_resume_and_get() so the register write only happens once
the device is confirmed active, matching the pattern already used
correctly elsewhere in the kernel (e.g. 8250_omap.c).

Signed-off-by: Cargt <tim.carney@cargt.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/tty/serial/stm32-usart.c b/drivers/tty/serial/stm32-usart.c
index 1111111111..2222222222 100644
--- a/drivers/tty/serial/stm32-usart.c
+++ b/drivers/tty/serial/stm32-usart.c
@@ -1341,7 +1341,20 @@ static void stm32_usart_break_ctl(struct uart_port *port, int break_state)
 	const struct stm32_usart_offsets *ofs = &stm32_port->info->ofs;
 	unsigned long flags;

-	pm_runtime_get(port->dev);
+	/*
+	 * pm_runtime_get() only requests an async resume and returns
+	 * immediately - it does not wait for the clock/power domain to
+	 * actually come back up. If this port has already autosuspended,
+	 * the register write below can then race a still-in-progress
+	 * resume and hit an unclocked peripheral, causing a synchronous
+	 * external abort. This has been observed triggered from
+	 * btnxpuart's power-save workqueue calling serdev_device_break_ctl()
+	 * asynchronously, with no other guarantee the port is already
+	 * resumed. Use the blocking variant so the register write only
+	 * happens once the device is confirmed active.
+	 */
+	if (pm_runtime_resume_and_get(port->dev) < 0)
+		return;

 	spin_lock_irqsave(&port->lock, flags);

